.TH CHANTEY 1 "2026-10-23" "chantey 0.7.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
chantey \- sheet-music pipeline: canonical MusicXML to sound, notation, and comparisons
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B chantey
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B chantey info
.I score
.RB [ \-\-sfz
.IR path ]
.br
.B chantey ingest
.I file
.RB [ \-o
.IR out.musicxml ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.br
.B chantey render
.I score.musicxml
.RB [ \-\-voice
.IR piano | synth | both ]
.RB [ \-\-engine
.IR fluidsynth | sfizz ]
.RB [ \-\-sfz
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-fx
.IR dry | light | default ]
.RB [ \-\-expression
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-pdf ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.br
.B chantey diff
.I original revised
.RB [ \-v ]
.br
.B chantey compare
.I original revised
.RB [ \-o
.IR out.html ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B chantey
turns written sheet music into things you can hear and compare. Its central
idea:
.B the PDF is a rendering, not the source
\(em one canonical MusicXML file per piece is the single source of truth,
and audio and notation are both renders of it. Edit the source and
everything regenerates consistently; compare two versions and the changed
measures are found automatically.
.PP
The intended workflow: obtain or transcribe a score into canonical
MusicXML; request a change in musical terms (\(lqthe chord in measures
12\-16\(rq, \(lqthat bass figure, everywhere it occurs\(rq); apply it as a
small music21 script; then use
.B diff
to confirm exactly which measures changed,
.B render
to hear both versions, and
.B compare
to see them engraved side by side with the changes highlighted.
.PP
chantey wraps existing engines rather than reimplementing them: Audiveris
(optical music recognition), MuseScore (PDF engraving), FluidSynth or
sfizz (piano voice), Verovio (SVG engraving), and
.BR hornpipe (1)
(its companion deterministic synthesizer).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "info " score " \fR[\fP\-\-sfz " path "\fR]\fP"
Parse and summarize: title/composer, parts with their measure counts and
lengths in quarters (as rendered, repeats expanded, which is the
.B \-\-expression
timeline; and as written when that differs), the render tail in quarters, key and time
signatures with their change points, tempo marks, note count, repeat
barlines and voltas. Run it first, always \(em it is the quick check that
an ingest worked and the reference map for edit requests.
Under each part whose SFZ is known (its
.B CHANTEY_SFZ_MAP
entry, else
.BR "\-\-sfz " path ),
it prints that SFZ's keyswitch map:
.BR sw_lokey \- sw_hikey ,
.BR sw_default ,
and each
.B sw_last
key with its
.BR sw_label ,
as MIDI keys with SFZ note names (c4 = 60). An SFZ that cannot be read is a
warning.
.TP
.BI "ingest " file " \fR[\fP\-o " out.musicxml "\fR]\fP [\-\-force]"
Produce the canonical MusicXML. For
.B .mxl
or
.B .musicxml
input (always preferred \(em lossless, nothing to proof), normalize to
uncompressed canonical form. For
.B .pdf
input, run Audiveris OMR headless (chantey supplies the flatpak
\-\-filesystem plumbing) and convert its export; a reminder of the known
OMR loss classes and the proofing procedure is printed. Metadata is
carried through; a missing title is backfilled from the filename.
Non\-clobbering.
.TP
.BI "render " score.musicxml " \fR[\fP\-\-voice " piano | synth | both "\fR]\fP \fR[\fP\-\-engine " fluidsynth | sfizz "\fR]\fP \fR[\fP\-\-sfz " path "\fR]\fP \fR[\fP\-\-fx " dry | light | default "\fR]\fP \fR[\fP\-\-expression " file "\fR]\fP [\-\-no\-pdf] [\-o " dir "\fR]\fP [\-\-force]"
Render audio and notation. Outputs land beside the score (or in
.IR dir ):
.IB stem .piano.wav
(MIDI through FluidSynth with a General MIDI SoundFont, dry by default,
see
.BR \-\-fx ;
or through sfizz with one SFZ per part, see
.BR \-\-engine ),
.IB stem .synth.toml
and
.IB stem .synth.wav
(a hornpipe score and its deterministic render), and
.IB stem .pdf
(MuseScore engraving, repeat notation preserved on the page). Default
voice is
.BR both .
Two guarantees: repeats and voltas are expanded once and identically for
both audio voices, so they can never disagree about the piece's form; and
the synth conversion normalizes all timing to seconds, so mid\-score tempo
changes work even though hornpipe itself is single\-tempo.
Non\-clobbering.
.IP
.B \-\-fx
sets the piano voice's FluidSynth effects.
.B dry
(the default) turns chorus and reverb off
.RB ( "\-C 0 \-R 0" ),
so stems carry no baked\-in space;
.B light
turns chorus off and adds a small, short reverb
.RB ( "\-C 0 \-o synth.reverb.active=1 \-o synth.reverb.level=0.3 \-o synth.reverb.room\-size=0.3" );
.B default
adds nothing and keeps FluidSynth's own defaults (chorus on, reverb level
0.9), as chantey 0.1 rendered. Arguments from
.B CHANTEY_FLUIDSYNTH_OPTS
follow the preset. The setting in effect is printed on the piano voice's
output line, for example
.BR "Wrote tune.piano.wav  [fx: dry]" .
The synth voice and the PDF are unaffected.
.IP
.B \-\-engine
selects the piano voice's engine.
.B fluidsynth
(the default) is the path above.
.B sfizz
renders each part through its own SFZ instrument: the score's MIDI is split
into one file per part (that part's events plus the tempo map), each is
rendered by
.B sfizz_render \-\-sfz
.I sfz
.B \-\-midi
.I mid
.B \-\-wav
.I wav
.BR "\-s 44100 \-b 256 \-q 10 \-\-use\-eot" ,
and the parts are summed at unity gain. A sum above
\-0.1 dBFS is scaled down to it and the factor printed.
Every part ends at the score's notated end plus 2 s (a text event at that
tick carries the tail, because sfizz_render 1.2.3 ignores an End\-of\-Track's
own delta), so a voice sfizz never releases cannot render forever. Each
sfizz_render also runs under a file\-size limit of twice that length's bytes
plus 1 MiB; reaching it stops the render and is an error naming the part,
with nothing written. Working files go in a
.I chantey\-sfizz\-*
directory under
.BR CHANTEY_TMPDIR ,
else
.BR TMPDIR ,
removed on success, on error and on SIGINT, SIGTERM or SIGHUP.
The SFZ for a part
comes from
.B CHANTEY_SFZ_MAP
by part\-name, else from
.BR "\-\-sfz " path ;
a part with neither is an error naming it, with no fallback to FluidSynth.
.B \-\-sfz
without
.B \-\-engine sfizz
is an error.
.B \-\-fx
and
.B CHANTEY_FLUIDSYNTH_OPTS
are ignored under sfizz, and the output line says so. The output line
records the engine, the mapping and the bound, for example
.BR "Wrote tune.piano.wav  [engine: sfizz; sfz: Trumpet=/lib/Trumpet.sfz, Tuba=/lib/Tuba.sfz; eot: +2.0 s; cap: 68.1 MB per part]" .
.IP
.BI "\-\-expression " file
merges per\-part controller curves, timing offsets (and, under sfizz, keyswitch notes) from a JSON sidecar into the piano
voice's MIDI, under either engine, before the engine runs:
.nf
.RS
{"parts": {"Trumpet": {"bend_range": 200, "curves": [
  {"cc": 11, "interp": "linear", "points": [[0, 90], [16, 127]]},
  {"cc": 64, "interp": "hold", "points": [[0, 0], [4, 127], [7.5, 0]]},
  {"bend": true, "interp": "linear", "points": [[32, 0], [32.5, \-100]]}]}}}
.RE
.fi
Parts are matched by part\-name as for
.BR CHANTEY_SFZ_MAP .
A curve names one kind:
.B cc
(0\-119; values 0\-127) or
.B bend
(values in cents within
.BR bend_range ,
default 200, which must match the instrument's). Points are
[offset, value], offsets in quarter lengths on the rendered timeline
(repeats expanded; see
.BR info ).
.B hold
sends one event per point;
.B linear
one per step between points, the step being 1/32 quarter or 10 ms,
whichever is coarser. The first value holds from 0, the last to the end, and
repeated values are dropped. Events go on the channels the part's notes
use, before a note\-on at the same tick and after everything else there.
An unknown part\-name, a value out of range, an offset past the part's
length plus the 2 s tail, unsorted offsets, or two curves on one controller
for one part is an error with nothing written. Under FluidSynth, a part
with curves that shares a MIDI channel with another part is an error.
.IP
A third kind,
.BR "{\(dqkeyswitch\(dq: true, \(dqinterp\(dq: \(dqhold\(dq, \(dqpoints\(dq: [[0, 36], [16, 39]]}" ,
picks an articulation in a keyswitch SFZ: each value is a MIDI key (0\-127),
sent as a note\-on (velocity 100, or the curve's
.BR \(dqvelocity\(dq ,
1\-127) and its note\-off one tick later, before the part's notes at that
tick. It is sent at its points only (before the first, the SFZ's
.B sw_default
plays), a point repeating the previous key is dropped, and
.B hold
is its only
.BR interp .
One keyswitch curve per part. A switch key inside the part's played range
(lowest to highest note) is an error naming the key and the nearest played
note. A keyswitch curve needs
.BR "\-\-engine sfizz" :
under FluidSynth it is an error, since a GM SoundFont would play the key as
a pitch.
.IP
A fourth kind,
.BR "{\(dqtime\(dq: true, \(dqinterp\(dq: \(dqlinear\(dq, \(dqpoints\(dq: [[0, 12], [64, 12], [68, \-5]]}" ,
moves every note\-on and note\-off of the part by its value in milliseconds
(positive is later) at that event's own written offset, converted at the
tempo there:
.B hold
takes the last point at or before the offset,
.B linear
the exact line between points. A note's on and off are read separately, so
its length changes by the difference. Values must lie within
.RB \(+- time_max ,
a part key (default 60, greater than 0, at most 1000). One time curve per
part. Controllers, keyswitches, program changes and tempi keep their ticks,
so a note written at a keyswitch and moved earlier plays before the switch.
Per key, a note moved before 0 is clamped to 0, a note\-off moved past the
next note\-on of its key is pulled back to it, and an event moved before
the previous event of its key is raised to it. Under FluidSynth a part with
a time curve that shares a MIDI channel is an error.
.IP
The piano line gains
.BR "[expression: " file "; parts: " n "; events: " m ]
(each switch counts as two events, its note\-on and note\-off), ending
.BR "; shifted: " s "; clamped: " c
when a part has a time curve (note events moved off their written tick, and
those a clamp moved);
the synth voice ignores the sidecar and its line says so. Without
.BR \-\-expression ,
or with only empty curves, the MIDI is unchanged.
.TP
.BI "diff " original " " revised " \fR[\fP\-v\fR]\fP"
Compare two versions measure by measure (note pitches, positions, and
durations, per part) and print the changed\-measure list \(em the lingua
franca for reporting an edit back (\(lqchanged mm. 7, 15, 56...\(rq).
.B \-v
adds per\-part detail. Identical scores are reported as such. Exit 0 either
way.
.TP
.BI "compare " original " " revised " \fR[\fP\-o " out.html "\fR]\fP [\-\-force]"
Emit the comparison page: one self\-contained local HTML file with both
versions engraved side by side (Verovio SVG),
.B every measure numbered,
the changed measures \(em found by
.BR diff ,
never hand\-listed \(em highlighted in crimson in both columns, and audio
players for whatever rendered WAVs already sit beside the scores (run
.B render
first). Identical scores produce a notice and no page. Non\-clobbering.
.SH EDITING
Deliberately not a command. Musical edits are short music21 scripts written
per request; every real request reduces to
.IR "match a shape, transform it, report the measures" .
The README carries a worked example (splitting a recurring bass figure into
twinned sixteenths, applied at all six occurrences the scan found). After
any edit, run
.B chantey diff
to confirm the changed measures before rendering.
.SH EXTERNAL PREREQUISITES
Checked at use; a missing one produces an actionable error, never a silent
skip.
.TP
.B Audiveris
PDF \(-> MusicXML (OMR). flatpak: first
.B flatpak remote\-add \-\-user \-\-if\-not\-exists flathub https://dl.flathub.org/repo/flathub.flatpakrepo
then
.BR "flatpak install \-y \-\-user flathub org.audiveris.audiveris" .
Only needed for PDF ingest.
.TP
.B mscore3
PDF engraving:
.BR "sudo apt install musescore3" .
Run headless by chantey (QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen).
.TP
.B fluidsynth
Piano voice:
.BR "sudo apt install fluidsynth fluid\-soundfont\-gm" .
.TP
.B sfizz_render
Piano voice under
.BR "\-\-engine sfizz" :
the sfztools
.B sfizz
package, plus the SFZ libraries to map. Only needed for that engine.
.TP
.B hornpipe
Deterministic synth voice:
.BR "uv tool install git+https://github.com/mr\-kelley/hornpipe" .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CHANTEY_SOUNDFONT
Path to the General MIDI SoundFont for the piano voice. Default
.IR /usr/share/sounds/sf2/FluidR3_GM.sf2 .
.TP
.B CHANTEY_FLUIDSYNTH_OPTS
Optional extra FluidSynth arguments for the piano voice, split shell\-style
and appended after the
.B \-\-fx
preset. chantey forwards them unchecked; their validity is the caller's
concern. They are printed with the fx setting on the output line.
Ignored under
.BR "\-\-engine sfizz" .
.TP
.B CHANTEY_SFZ_MAP
Per\-part SFZ instruments for
.BR "\-\-engine sfizz" :
.IR "part\-name" = path ; part\-name = path ...
Part names are the MusicXML
.I part\-name
text, matched exactly first, then case\-insensitively. The map wins per
part;
.B \-\-sfz
fills the rest. An entry that matches no part is a warning.
.TP
.B CHANTEY_TMPDIR
Directory for
.B \-\-engine sfizz
working files (per\-part MIDI and stems); point it at a big filesystem for
long scores. Unset:
.BR TMPDIR ,
else the system default. A value naming no directory is an error.
.SH OMR PROOFING
Audiveris is good on clean born\-digital engravings, workable on scans,
poor on photos. After a PDF ingest, always proof: render the result and
compare against the original by eye and ear. Known loss classes: tempo and
expression text, fingerings (occasionally misread as dynamics),
articulation glyphs. Fix what matters in the canonical MusicXML \(em it is
the source now. Born\-digital MusicXML, when it exists, skips all of this.
.SH LICENSED MATERIAL
Much sheet music is copyrighted, including modern arrangements of
public\-domain tunes. chantey is built for private, local work on lawfully
obtained copies: it never publishes, uploads, or hosts anything \(em the
comparison page is a local file. Keep working scores and derived artifacts
in a directory excluded from version control (a gitignored
.IR scratch/ ,
say)
so licensed material never enters history.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (including \(lqidentical scores\(rq outcomes).
.TP
.B 1
Error: unparseable score, missing binary, failed external tool, refused
overwrite.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 128 + n
Stopped by signal
.I n
(SIGINT, SIGTERM or SIGHUP) during an sfizz render, after its working
files were removed.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
.RS
chantey ingest tune.mxl                # \(-> tune.musicxml (canonical)
chantey info tune.musicxml
#   ...apply the requested edit as a music21 script \(-> revised.musicxml...
chantey diff tune.musicxml revised.musicxml
chantey render tune.musicxml
chantey render revised.musicxml
chantey compare tune.musicxml revised.musicxml
#   open compare.html in a browser
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hornpipe (1),
.BR fluidsynth (1),
and the Texinfo manual
.RB ( "info chantey" ),
which covers the workflow and concepts in tutorial form.
